\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Temporal DIF as Structured Signal}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF detects \emph{that} items function differently across cohorts, not \emph{why}---and this is by design (``temporal'' denotes release-year cohorts that differ simultaneously in architecture, data, and methodology; \Cref{sec:cohorts}). Stratifying by model type reveals opposite patterns: base models ($N{=}1{,}409$) are 60.1\% reference-favoring ($\rho = -0.570$), while instruct models ($N{=}478$) are 65.6\% focal-favoring ($\rho = -0.079$; $\chi^2 = 562.7$, $p < 10^{-124}$). Following the impact-bias framework~\citep{camilli1994methods}, this reversal reflects structured item-level bias rather than uniform group impact, validating DIF as a mechanism-agnostic diagnostic (\Cref{app:mechanism_discussion}).

\looseness=-1
Composition downsampling ($\Delta = 1.08$\,pp) and base-only/instruct-only temporal analyses confirm DIF is not an artifact of cohort structure (\Cref{app:composition_control,app:baseinst_temporal,app:within_arch}).

\looseness=-2
Three features distinguish LLM temporal DIF from educational settings: (1)~C\% (29.2\%; cluster bootstrap CI: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$) substantially exceeds typical educational DIF rates~\citep{clauser1998dif}; (2)~DIF flags and web-overlap labels are near-independent (Jaccard $= 0.206$), unlike educational settings where DIF correlates with construct-irrelevant variance; (3)~the near-independence admits two interpretations: DIF captures a broader signal---including capability evolution and training methodology shifts---of which contamination is one component, or the two measure distinct constructs. The base/instruct reversal (OR $= 4.75$; \Cref{app:mechanism_discussion}) favors the former; either interpretation supports periodic DIF audits.

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

\looseness=-2
Item-level properties do not predict DIF susceptibility (AUROC $= 0.60$; \Cref{app:predictive}), precluding pre-screening. We recommend a three-step protocol: (1)~\emph{Monitor}: run MH-DIF audits whenever ${\geq}$500 models accumulate ($K{=}3$ for smaller corpora; \Cref{tab:k3_power}), triggering review when C\% exceeds $20\times$ the random-split baseline (C\% $\approx 12\%$ for MMLU); (2)~\emph{Triage}: apply mechanism-conditional responses---DIF $\times$ web-overlap co-occurrence warrants removal; DIF on web-clean items warrants monitoring (DIF alone cannot determine causation); base/instruct reversal motivates refined cohort definitions; (3)~\emph{Correct}: DIF flags identify items warranting expert review, not automatic removal---DIF is necessary but not sufficient for item bias~\citep{camilli1994methods,penfield2007dif}. Report DIF-corrected scores alongside raw scores (\Cref{app:score_inflation}), or apply equating adjustments~\citep{kolen2014equating,habba2026growingpains,suk2026rethinking}.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark validation across all six METABENCH benchmarks appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

\looseness=-1
A Monte Carlo power analysis (1,000 replications) using empirical 2PL parameters from PSN-IRT~\citep{zhou2026psnirt} ($\bar{a} = 1.2$, $\bar{b} = -0.84$) confirms that at $N \geq 100$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80 (under uniform injection) and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$ (\Cref{app:method_details}). Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold; power varies with item discrimination (TPR 0.40--0.95 across $a = 0.5$--$2.0$; \Cref{app:power_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Temporal DIF: ${\sim}$29\% of Items Exhibit Differential Functioning}
\label{sec:temporal}

\looseness=-1
We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$; bootstrap 95\% CI: $[29.2\%, 37.8\%]$; family-level cluster bootstrap: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$, upper bound driven by mega-family over-representation; \Cref{app:uncertainty}). Two design-effect (DEFF) estimators bound the non-independence effect: simple-mean DEFF yields 29.2\% and weighted-mean DEFF yields 13.5\%, bracketing temporal C\% at 13.5--29.2\%. The cluster bootstrap 95\% CI ($[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$)---a family-level resampling method independent of DEFF methodology---excludes the weighted-mean estimate (13.5\%), providing model-independent evidence that over-correction drives the lower bound. Under weighted-mean DEFF (C\% $= 13.5\%$), all downstream conclusions hold: $\rho = 0.999$, score shift $= {+}1.77$\,pp (\Cref{app:deff,app:deff_simulation,app:icc_family}). Under 100 random permutations within the 2023 cohort (540 vs.\ 540), the 99th percentile of item-level $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|$ is 1.45---just below the ETS~C threshold of 1.5---confirming that the threshold maintains a per-item false-positive rate below 1\%; together with the Monte Carlo power analysis (TPR $\geq 0.68$; \Cref{sec:validation}), this validates ETS~C classification for the LLM setting. No non-dominant family drives the signal (LOFO $|\Delta\mathrm{C\%}| \leq 2.93$\,pp for families ${\leq}15\%$ of cohort; dominant-family drops reflect composition, not signal; \Cref{tab:lofo}); graduated de-duplication confirms C\% scales with power, not redundancy (\Cref{app:dedup_k}).

\looseness=-1
Three controls confirm genuine temporal change (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): random splits yield mean C\% of 0.62\% (2023; 100 permutations) and 1.3\% (2024; 100 permutations), 24--50$\times$ below temporal C\%; ability-quintile comparison yields 2.9\%; and 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on total score ($N{=}638$/group, Cohen's $d = -0.17$) yields C\% $= 28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$, confirming at most ${\sim}2.4$~pp is attributable to ability mismatch ($p < 0.01$; \Cref{app:method_details}).

\looseness=-1
Across all 57 subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). DIF rates are domain-invariant (Kruskal-Wallis $p = 0.78$), but DIF \emph{direction} exhibits domain dependence: STEM items favor 2024 models (55.4\%), Humanities favor 2023 (64.4\%). DIF is item-specific: domain-level accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (\Cref{app:structural}). A difficulty--direction interaction emerges ($\rho = -0.597$): 2024 models gain on hard items while eroding on easy ones. The temporal DIF finding persists across thresholds ($18.4\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$).

